[2026-03-16T01:35:15.430Z] {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Qf4dUr9yN3TA9eLx6ZRfsv","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Skill\n     2→\n     3→You are writing playwright tests which check that all the different entries in docs/tests.md are satisfied by the app.\n     4→\n     5→## Unpack Subtasks\n     6→\n     7→Unpack the initial write tests task into subtasks using `add-task`. Add ALL page tasks in a\n     8→single `add-task` call, with one task per page containing all test entries for that page.\n     9→Write out every task explicitly — do not use a loop or script:\n    10→\n    11→```bash\n    12→npx tsx /repo/scripts/add-task.ts <<'EOF'\n    13→[\n    14→  { \"skill\": \"skills/tasks/build/writeTests.md\", \"app\": \"<AppName>\", \"subtasks\": [\n    15→    \"WriteTest<TestEntry1>: Write test for <TestEntry1>\",\n    16→    \"WriteTest<TestEntry2>: Write test for <TestEntry2>\"\n    17→  ]},\n    18→  { \"skill\": \"skills/tasks/build/writeTests.md\", \"app\": \"<AppName>\", \"subtasks\": [\n    19→    \"WriteTest<TestEntry3>: Write test for <TestEntry3>\"\n    20→  ]}\n    21→]\n    22→EOF\n    23→```\n    24→\n    25→## Guidelines\n    26→\n    27→- For a given test entry, write a playwright test which matches the entry's requirements and verifies that the app will behave as the user expects.\n    28→- Do not run the playwright tests, but make sure that the test should pass and update the app code if necessary.\n    29→- The title of the playwright test must match the title of the test entry.\n    30→- If you discover parts of the app that haven't been fully implemented, you must finish implementation of the app.\n    31→- Playwright tests and app components/pages must use data-testid to identify elements on the page.\n    32→\n    33→## Directives\n    34→\n    35→- Tests must verify navigation targets precisely — assert the URL or page content after a click, not just that a click handler exists. A link going to the wrong page is a common bug that only surfaces if the test checks where it actually lands.\n    36→\n    37→- When a test spec entry implies a component is available across multiple pages (e.g., a sequential navigation flow that clicks sidebar links after navigating away from the starting page), tests must interact with that component on each page it should appear on. Never use `page.goto()` to navigate back to a known page as a workaround for a missing component — this masks the bug instead of catching it. If the spec says \"click X in the sidebar\" after navigating to a different page, the test must click the sidebar on that page, not reload the original page first.\n    38→\n    39→- For actions that produce side effects (e.g. history entries, timeline updates), write assertions that verify both the primary effect and the side effect. Also assert the side effect happens exactly once — duplicate entries from redundant API calls are a common bug.\n    40→\n    41→- When an action modifies child records (e.g., adding, editing, or removing line items, entries, or sub-rows), the test must also assert that any parent-level computed/aggregate values (e.g., total cost, item count, running balance) are updated correctly in the UI. Backend endpoints that modify children may forget to recalculate the parent aggregate, so only an explicit assertion on the updated parent value will catch this.\n    42→\n    43→- Avoid using `getByText()` or `filter({ hasText })` with common words that may appear as substrings\n    44→  in other elements (labels, options, buttons). Both Playwright's `getByText` and `filter({ hasText })`\n    45→  use case-insensitive substring matching by default — e.g., `hasText: 'Male'` also matches \"Female\".\n    46→  Prefer `getByTestId` for precise element targeting, or use `getByRole`/`getByLabel` with exact matching.\n    47→  When using `filter({ hasText })`, pass a regex with anchors (e.g., `{ hasText: /^Male$/ }`) for exact matching\n    48→  ONLY on leaf elements that contain just the target text. On composite elements (cards, rows, list items)\n    49→  whose `textContent` includes text from multiple children (name, category, status, etc.), anchored regex\n    50→  will fail because it matches against the full concatenated text. For composite elements, use\n    51→  `filter({ has: page.locator('[data-testid=\"child-element\"]', { hasText: /^exact text$/ }) })` to target\n    52→  a specific child element instead.\n    53→  A `strict mode violation: getByText(...) resolved to N elements` error means the selector is ambiguous —\n    54→  never work around it with `.first()`, instead use a more specific selector like `getByTestId` or\n    55→  `getByRole` with `{ exact: true }`.\n    56→\n    57→- All `data-testid` values used across all test files for the same component must be consistent. Before\n    58→  writing cross-cutting tests that reference components tested in other spec files, read the existing\n    59→  spec files to use the same testid values.\n    60→\n    61→- When the test spec entry describes file upload functionality (e.g., \"file picker opens\", \"select a\n    62→  file to upload\"), the test must exercise actual file upload mechanics (e.g., Playwright's\n    63→  `setInputFiles` on a file input). Do not substitute a URL/text input test for a file upload test —\n    64→  this masks missing upload functionality in the app.\n    65→\n    66→- NEVER put Playwright locator calls with auto-wait semantics (e.g. `count()`, `textContent()`,\n    67→  `isVisible()`) inside a `.toPass()` retry block in a way that iterates over dynamic elements.\n    68→  If the DOM changes mid-iteration, an inner locator call will auto-wait for an element that no\n    69→  longer exists, and `.toPass()` cannot interrupt that inner wait to retry the block — the test\n    70→  deadlocks. Instead, use a single atomic Playwright assertion that has built-in retry:\n    71→\n    72→  BAD — nested waits deadlock when DOM changes mid-loop:\n    73→  ```ts\n    74→  await expect(async () => {\n    75→    const cards = page.locator('[data-testid^=\"card-\"]');\n    76→    const count = await cards.count();\n    77→    for (let i = 0; i < count; i++) {\n    78→      const text = await cards.nth(i).textContent(); // hangs if DOM shrinks\n    79→      if (text?.includes(name)) found = true;\n    80→    }\n    81→    expect(found).toBeFalsy();\n    82→  }).toPass({ timeout: 15000 });\n    83→  ```\n    84→\n    85→  GOOD — single atomic assertion with built-in retry, no nested waits:\n    86→  ```ts\n    87→  await expect(\n    88→    page.locator('[data-testid^=\"card-\"]').filter({ hasText: name })\n    89→  ).toHaveCount(0, { timeout: 15000 });\n    90→  ```\n    91→\n    92→  The same principle applies to any `.toPass()` block: keep the body free of Playwright auto-waiting\n    93→  calls that can block indefinitely. Use locator chaining (`.filter()`, `.locator()`) and\n    94→  single-assertion expect matchers (`.toHaveCount()`, `.toContainText()`, `.toBeVisible()`) instead.\n    95→\n    96→- Every test file MUST include a `beforeAll` hook that calls `truncateAndSeed` to ensure a\n    97→  clean database state before the spec runs. This is boilerplate — include it in every new\n    98→  spec file without exception. Cross-run data accumulation (records persisting from prior\n    99→  test executions against the same Neon branch) is the single largest failure sub-category,\n   100→  accounting for 40% of all test failures in one session. The `beforeAll` with\n   101→  `truncateAndSeed` prevents this entire class of failures.\n   102→\n   103→- Every test file that modifies database records MUST also include a `beforeEach` cleanup\n   104→  helper that resets the relevant data via API calls. Do not wait for data contamination\n   105→  failures to surface before adding cleanup — add it from the start during test authoring.\n   106→  This is the #1 cause of test difficulty, accounting for 24%+ of all test failures. The\n   107→  cleanup helper should fetch ALL records of the relevant type and delete each one, rather\n   108→  than deleting a hardcoded list of known IDs.\n   109→\n   110→- Do not add unnecessary state cleanup (e.g., `localStorage.removeItem`, `page.reload()`) in\n   111→  `beforeEach` hooks when Playwright already provides a fresh browser context per test. Redundant\n   112→  cleanup wastes time and can cause tests to exceed their timeout under recording or CI overhead.\n   113→\n   114→- For assertions that depend on backend round-trips (auth flows, database writes, API calls),\n   115→  use generous timeouts (e.g., `{ timeout: 30000 }`) rather than tight ones. Environments with\n   116→  recording overhead (Replay browser) add significant latency beyond typical local development.\n   117→  A tight timeout that barely passes locally will flake under load or recording.\n   118→\n   119→- For tests that chain multiple user flows in a single test (e.g., signup → signout → signin →\n   120→  verify), add `test.slow()` at the top of the test to triple the default timeout. Multi-step\n   121→  end-to-end flows easily exceed the default 60s timeout under recording and CI environments.\n   122→\n   123→- Skill files are at `/repo/skills/tasks/` and its subdirectories (the repo root), NOT inside\n   124→  the app directory. Always use `/repo/skills/tasks/build/writeTests.md`, etc.\n   125→\n   126→- For deployment tests (tests run against a live deployed site rather than a local dev server):\n   127→  - Always use `data-testid` values that match the actual component markup. Read the component\n   128→    source to verify testid values before writing the test — do not guess.\n   129→  - Handle empty-state scenarios gracefully. A freshly deployed app may have no data, so tests\n   130→    must not assume specific records exist. Check for empty-state UI (e.g., \"No items\" messages)\n   131→    or create test data before asserting on table/list contents.\n   132→  - Use generous timeouts (e.g., `{ timeout: 10000 }`) for element assertions. First-load\n   133→    performance on a cold deploy is slower than local development.\n   134→  - Verify that API endpoints are healthy before running deployment tests. See\n   135→    `skills/scripts/deploy-verification.md`.\n   136→\n   137→- **Avoid `.or()` locator patterns that can match multiple elements.** Playwright's `.or()` combinator\n   138→  creates a locator that matches elements from either branch. When both branches resolve to elements on\n   139→  the page, the combined locator matches multiple elements and causes strict-mode violations. For example,\n   140→  `page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Save' }).or(page.getByTestId('save-btn'))` will fail if both\n   141→  a button named \"Save\" and an element with testid `save-btn` exist (or are the same element matched\n   142→  differently). Prefer a single, specific locator (`getByTestId` or `getByRole` with exact matching)\n   143→  over `.or()` fallback chains.\n   144→\n   145→- When using CSS attribute prefix selectors like `[data-testid^=\"prefix-\"]` to count or collect\n   146→  elements, verify that the prefix does not also match child elements with longer testid values\n   147→  that share the same prefix. For example, `[data-testid^=\"group-\"]` will match both `group-foo`\n   148→  and `group-header-foo`. Either use a more specific selector, add a `:not()` exclusion, or\n   149→  use exact `getByTestId` calls to target only the intended elements.\n   150→\n   151→- When testing that an action adds or removes items from a list or table, assert relative changes\n   152→  (e.g., count increased by 1) rather than hardcoding absolute expected counts. Capture the initial\n   153→  count before the action and assert the new count equals `initialCount + 1` (or `- 1` for deletion).\n   154→  Absolute counts couple the test to seed data and break when prior tests or setup changes alter the\n   155→  starting state.\n   156→\n   157→- Never hardcode time-dependent values (dates, timestamps) in test assertions. If a test spec\n   158→  says a field defaults to \"today\" or \"now\", the test must compute the expected value dynamically\n   159→  (e.g., `new Date().toISOString().split('T')[0]`) rather than hardcoding a specific date string.\n   160→  Hardcoded dates cause the test to fail on any day other than the one when the test was written.\n   161→\n   162→- When asserting that a data field (e.g., totalPaid, balance) has been updated, verify it in\n   163→  the view that actually renders that field. Read the component source to confirm which fields\n   164→  are displayed in list/table views vs. detail views. A common bug is asserting on a table row\n   165→  that only shows a subset of fields (e.g., totalCharged) when the expected field (e.g., totalPaid)\n   166→  is only visible in the detail view.\n   167→\n   168→- When a test spec entry describes editing or interacting with a specific field (e.g., \"edit client\n   169→  field\", \"change value\"), the test must exercise that exact field with corresponding actions and\n   170→  assertions. Do not write a test that only verifies a subset of the fields or operations mentioned\n   171→  in the spec entry — each described interaction must be tested. A common bug is implementing editing\n   172→  for some fields but not all; only per-field testing catches this.\n   173→\n   174→- Apps with login/signup functionality must have a complete e2e test that exercises the full\n   175→  sign-up and sign-in flow against the real auth backend. The test must create a new account,\n   176→  verify the post-signup state (session established or confirmation required), then sign in\n   177→  with the new credentials and verify the authenticated state. This catches issues like email\n   178→  confirmation requirements, incorrect error handling, and session establishment failures that\n   179→  unit-level or mocked tests miss.\n   180→\n   181→- When the spec says a value is automatically derived or auto-selected (e.g., \"defaults to today\",\n   182→  \"auto-selects the current user\"), the test must verify that the auto-selection works on its own —\n   183→  navigate to the page and assert the correct value is already selected without manually choosing it.\n   184→  If the test manually selects the value before asserting, it bypasses the auto-selection code path\n   185→  and will not catch bugs like format mismatches or incorrect comparisons.\n   186→\n   187→- When a test spec entry says a value should be displayed in a specific format (e.g., a formatted\n   188→  date like \"Mon, Mar 4\", a currency like \"$51.50\"), the test must assert on the actual text content\n   189→  or input value of the element, not just that the element is visible or present. Checking only\n   190→  visibility will not catch rendering bugs where the value is malformed (e.g., \"Invalid Date\",\n   191→  \"$NaN\", \"undefined\"). Use `.toHaveText()` or `.toContainText()` for displayed text, and\n   192→  `.toHaveValue()` for input fields, with the expected formatted value or a regex pattern. For\n   193→  input fields that format values (e.g., monetary inputs formatting \"130\" as \"130.00\"), the\n   194→  assertion must match the formatted value exactly, not just the numeric portion.\n   195→\n   196→- Always generate unique test data names by including `Date.now()` or `crypto.randomUUID()`\n   197→  suffixes (e.g., `Test Customer ${Date.now()}` instead of `Test Customer`). Non-unique test\n   198→  data causes strict-mode violations and cross-test contamination when multiple tests create\n   199→  records with identical names. This is a template requirement for all test data creation.\n   200→\n   201→- When a test file's describe block contains tests that perform destructive mid-test operations\n   202→  (e.g., deleting records via API requests during the test body) that would invalidate the\n   203→  preconditions of other tests in the same block, wrap those tests in `test.describe.serial`\n   204→  to guarantee sequential execution. With `fullyParallel: true` in the Playwright config,\n   205→  tests within a plain `test.describe` can run concurrently or in any order, so a destructive\n   206→  test may execute before or alongside tests that depend on the destroyed data.\n   207→\n   208→- When multiple tests in the same describe block perform state-changing operations on database\n   209→  records (e.g., submitting a claim, changing a status), each test must operate on a distinct\n   210→  record ID. Never have two tests mutate the same record, even if they run sequentially —\n   211→  the second test's preconditions will be invalid because the first test already changed the\n   212→  record's state. Use different seed data records for each test that performs mutations.\n   213→\n   214→- When a user action triggers a page navigation or data re-fetch (e.g., clicking a link that\n   215→  navigates to a new page, changing a date range filter, selecting a different category,\n   216→  submitting a search), or when a page loads data asynchronously on mount, the test must wait\n   217→  for the data to appear in the UI before making assertions. Do not assert on element counts\n   218→  or text content immediately after the action or page load — the DOM may still be in a\n   219→  loading state, show stale data, or display initial/default values (e.g., \"0\") before the\n   220→  API response arrives. In particular, never use `locator.count()` or `locator.textContent()`\n   221→  immediately to assert on dynamic content — these are snapshots that return instantly without\n   222→  waiting. Use auto-retrying assertions like `toHaveText()`, `toHaveCount()`, or\n   223→  `toContainText()` instead. For example, use `await expect(el).toHaveText('5')` rather than\n   224→  `const text = await el.textContent(); expect(Number(text)).toBe(5)`. Alternatively, use\n   225→  `expect(...).toPass()`, wait for a specific element to be visible, or wait for a loading\n   226→  indicator to disappear before asserting on the rendered data.\n   227→\n   228→- When asserting on values derived from seed data (e.g., sums, counts, aggregates computed by\n   229→  the backend), verify the expected value by tracing through the actual seed data records and the\n   230→  query/computation that produces the displayed value. Do not guess or approximate — compute the\n   231→  expected value from the seed data to avoid arithmetic errors such as off-by-one mistakes.\n   232→\n   233→- Never use positional selectors (`.nth()`, `.first()`, `.last()`) to target specific rows or\n   234→  items when asserting on their content. Row ordering depends on backend sort order (often by\n   235→  UUID or insertion order), which may not match the order listed in the test spec entry. Instead,\n   236→  use content-based locators such as `rows.filter({ has: page.locator(...) })` to find the row\n   237→  containing the expected data, then assert on that row's contents. This ensures the test validates\n   238→  the correct data regardless of display order.\n   239→\n   240→- When a test changes a selection (e.g., picks a different item from a dropdown) and then\n   241→  performs a dependent action (e.g., deletes a row, checks a summary), the test must assert\n   242→  that the selection is still the expected value before proceeding with the dependent action.\n   243→  Async effects (especially under React StrictMode double-mount) can reset selections after\n   244→  the user has changed them. If the test does not re-verify the selection, it may silently\n   245→  operate on the wrong data, passing when the app is actually broken.\n   246→\n   247→- When asserting on URL query parameters in navigation tests, do not use `encodeURIComponent()`\n   248→  to build expected URL patterns. `encodeURIComponent` encodes spaces as `%20`, but apps that\n   249→  use `URLSearchParams` to construct query strings encode spaces as `+` (per the\n   250→  `application/x-www-form-urlencoded` spec). Instead, construct the expected URL using the same\n   251→  `URLSearchParams` API the app uses, or use a regex that accounts for both `+` and `%20` encoding.\n   252→\n   253→- When a test involves changing a parent selection that triggers auto-fill of dependent fields\n   254→  (e.g., switching from Patient A to Patient B auto-fills the insurance dropdown), the test must\n   255→  verify that the dependent fields are correctly populated with the NEW parent's data before\n   256→  proceeding. Stale data from the previous selection can remain in the store while new data is\n   257→  being fetched, causing auto-fill to silently select outdated values. Assert the dependent\n   258→  field's value matches the new parent's expected data, not just that a value is present.\n   259→\n   260→- Auth flows that involve email-based verification (email confirmation, password reset) must\n   261→  have dedicated tests that exercise the real production code path — not the IS_TEST bypass.\n   262→  These tests must run the backend without IS_TEST=true (or with IS_TEST=false) so that actual\n   263→  tokens are generated and stored. The test should call the signup/forgot-password endpoint,\n   264→  query the database directly for the generated token, then hit the confirmation/reset endpoint\n   265→  with that token and verify success. This ensures the full flow works end-to-end: token\n   266→  generation, storage, URL construction, and redemption. Other (non-auth-flow) tests may\n   267→  continue to use IS_TEST=true to bypass auth for convenience.\n   268→\n   269→## Test Design and Database Isolation\n   270→\n   271→Tests run serially (`--workers 1`) against a single `netlify dev` server backed by an ephemeral\n   272→Neon branch. The test script resets the database (truncate + re-seed) between each test.\n   273→\n   274→### Test infrastructure\n   275→\n   276→Do **not** use Playwright's `webServer` config to start the dev server. The test script manages\n   277→the `netlify dev` server lifecycle automatically.\n   278→\n   279→### Database access in functions\n   280→\n   281→All Netlify functions must use the shared `netlify/functions/db.ts` module (see writeApp.md\n   282→directives) rather than importing `@neondatabase/serverless` directly.\n   283→\n   284→### Test design rules\n   285→\n   286→- The database is reset between each test, so each test starts with a clean seed.\n   287→- Every test file that touches the database MUST rely on the test script's between-test DB\n   288→  reset (truncate + re-seed). If the test script's reset is insufficient for a specific\n   289→  scenario, add an explicit `beforeEach` hook that performs additional cleanup via API calls.\n   290→  Missing DB cleanup between tests is the #1 cause of test isolation failures and retries.\n   291→- Every test file that creates records (tasks, orders, customers, etc.) should include a\n   292→  `beforeEach` cleanup helper (e.g., `deleteAllTasks()`) that removes all records of the\n   293→  relevant type via API before each test. Add this from the start when writing tests — do\n   294→  not wait for data contamination failures to surface. This is a template requirement, not\n   295→  an optimization.\n   296→- Never hardcode database IDs in tests — including in cleanup and setup helpers. Query the\n   297→  UI or API to discover IDs for the records you need to interact with. Cleanup helpers that\n   298→  delete records must fetch ALL records of that type via the API and delete each one, rather\n   299→  than deleting a hardcoded list of known seed IDs. Hardcoded ID lists silently miss records\n   300→  created by prior tests, breaking data isolation.\n   301→- Each spec file must contain **20 or fewer** `test()` calls. When a page has more tests,\n   302→  split them across multiple spec files grouped by feature area (e.g., `status-page-containers.spec.ts`,\n   303→  `status-page-webhook.spec.ts`). Consolidate similar tests where possible before splitting.\n   304→- The Playwright config must use `fullyParallel: true`. Do not set `workers: 1`.\n   305→- The Playwright config must **not** include a `webServer` section — the worker fixture handles\n   306→  server lifecycle.\n   307→- The Playwright config must use `replayDevices['Replay Chromium']` from `@replayio/playwright`\n   308→  as the browser project, not standard `devices['Desktop Chrome']`. Tests must run under the\n   309→  Replay browser so that recordings are captured for debugging.\n   310→- Set the Playwright config's global `timeout` to at least 60000ms.\n   311→  The Replay browser has significant recording overhead, and with parallel workers all hitting\n   312→  their dev servers simultaneously, pages can take 25+ seconds to load. A 30s timeout that works\n   313→  locally with standard Chrome will cause widespread flakes under recording.\n   314→\n   315→## Playwright Config Selection\n   316→\n   317→Apps may have multiple Playwright config files for different purposes. Use the correct config\n   318→for each test type:\n   319→\n   320→- **`playwright.config.ts`** — Default config for local development tests. Used by `npm run test`.\n   321→  This is the config you should use for all `FixTests` and `WriteTests` workflows.\n   322→- **`playwright.deployment.config.ts`** — For deployment verification tests that run against a\n   323→  live deployed URL. Only use after `npm run deploy` has completed successfully.\n   324→- **`playwright.journey.config.ts`** — For JourneyQA end-to-end journey tests that validate\n   325→  full user flows. Used during JourneyQA tasks.\n   326→\n   327→When running tests via `npm run test`, the test script uses the default `playwright.config.ts`\n   328→automatically. Do not pass `--config` to override this unless you specifically need a different\n   329→config (e.g., deployment testing).\n   330→\n   331→## Tips\n   332→\n   333→- Before writing tests that interact with shared UI components (ConfirmDialog, modals, dropdowns),\n   334→  read the shared component first to get the exact `data-testid` values. Do not guess test IDs.\n   335→- When adding `data-testid` attributes to a set of components for a page, plan ALL testid additions\n   336→  upfront by reading all components first, then make edits in batches. This avoids repeated re-reads\n   337→  and sequential small edits to the same file.\n   338→- When writing tests for a detail page, read: (1) the Netlify function for the entity, (2) the page\n   339→  component, (3) 2-3 representative section components. Do not exhaustively read every file in the app.\n   340→- If Playwright browser installation fails with permission errors, set `PLAYWRIGHT_BROWSERS_PATH` to\n   341→  a writable directory (e.g., `/home/node/.cache/ms-playwright`) before running `npx playwright install`.\n   342→- When testing custom select/dropdown components (e.g., `CustomSelect`), standard Playwright\n   343→  `selectOption()` will not work. Instead, click the dropdown trigger to open it, then click\n   344→  the option using its `data-value` attribute (e.g., `page.click('[data-value=\"option1\"]')`).\n   345→  Similarly, `toHaveValue()` does not work on custom selects — use\n   346→  `getAttribute('data-value')` to verify the selected value.\n   347→- **NEVER invoke `npx playwright test` directly.** Always use `npm run test <file>` from the\n   348→  app directory. Direct `npx playwright test` invocations skip essential setup (dev server\n   349→  startup, ephemeral database branch creation, schema init, seeding) and fail ~80% of the time.\n   350→  The `npm run test` script handles the full server and database lifecycle automatically.\n   351→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_015mHckA24UDTvDqWytokBhE","session_id":"d8954074-381a-4a26-bc92-1002d48c1ee8","uuid":"2b7b9291-af2f-40c8-bcb3-67f45f3ce20f"}
